The way I see it, Compaq has thrown in the towel, admitting that it was
incapable of competing against DELL (and I fear the asians might be coming to
take over the desktop market), and that its Unix offering just isn't taking
any significant marketshare. The thing to do in this situation is to give
shareholders the most money/value for their shares.

I think that the primary goal for HP is to eliminate a customer. And with that
project, HP gains some key customers such as stock exchanges. The fact that HP
announced so quickly that the "Compaq" brand name would not survive is an
indication that HP doesn't care about Compaq's image in the low end market. If
HP wants to distance itself from the PC "box maker" image, it is good to drop
the "Compaq" name.

I don't see Tandem or VMS as being strategic to HP. It will keep them because
the cost of dropping them (PR, image etc) is too high. You can't drop the
stock exchange where you stock is trading !

One key asset of Compaq is its VMS intellectual property,  something which
Compaq has squandered. Not sure if/how HP would take advantage of it. They may
barter VMS in exchange for something else.

> My own personal view is that the deal allows Compaq to gain significant
> assistance in porting stuff to IPF.

<colourful expression>
Compaq doesn't need to port anything important to IPF now. The only porting
project that may not be canned is the VMS one, and that one is just for show
to give the impression that VMS isn't being killed. (The remaining customer
base will most probably remain on Alpha for many years to come since they
don't want to be bothered with a port to IA64 that would last only a few years
before VMS is officially declared dead in about 10 years.


