routine to run.  For all I know you could shorten some of the elements
of the boot command enough to get it to fit in 80 columns some other
way.

> Being a Unixoid from way back and a relatively recent (couple of years)
> VMS Sysadmin, I can honestly say if Linux were the only Unix I would
> only run VMS.

VMS is the best O/S ever made, period.  I sure wish the whole world knew
that.

I ran OSF/1...er Digital Unix on a bunch of 2100's and 4100's a few
years ago and I have to admit it was pretty darn good, for a Unix.  That
AdvFS is like magic!

My son can blunder his way around VMS because that's where he first
learned how to write C programs.  But what would be the point of
teaching him how to administer VMS?!  It won't exist for the most part
by the time he could use those skills in a career.  Now it seems the
same will be true of Tru64.  So Linux it is.  And from what I've seen of
it so far it's not at all a bad personal O/S.  No idea if it's ready to
take on the tasks I used to ask of Digital Unix (business-critical
server stuff) but I could guess the answer is "not yet."

Duncan


