In article <3BA49ED9.3BDBA9A1@ics.com>, James E Kogler <jimk@ics.com> writes:
>From: James E Kogler <jimk@ics.com>
>Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
>Subject: figuring out how much memory/Swap on True64.
>
>I have a true 64 system running i think 4.0e
>i am having problems building gcc-3.0.1 becasue i run out of virtual
>memory:
>
> [ Build example deleted... ]
>
>Virtual memory exhausted: Not enough space
>gmake[3]: *** [locale-inst.lo] Error 1
>
>
>i am just trying to figure out, how much swap space i have, how much
>physical memory etc..

You can use the swapon(8) command to see how page/swap space is
configured and how much there is.  The amount of memory is recorded
in the system boot messages which are written to /var/adm/messages
but this isn't generally world readable.  The amount of physical
memory would probably not have an affect on your symtom though;
it "just" keeps down the amount of paging necessary to use the
amount of virtual memory you want.

One common cause of this symtom is trying to an amount of stack
or data space in excess of the per-process limits.  The system
has two values for each, the default value and the maximum.  The
usual default value for the datasize is 128 MB (maximum 1 GB)
which is often not enough for some applications.  Most shells
support a built-in command that allows raising the default.  See
your shell manual page to see what it supports.

