The victims, though unfortunate, did not do anything heroic.  They did
not die for freedom and liberty, except perhaps some of those on United
flight 93, who may have acted to prevent a worse tragedy.  They died
because terrorists wanted to destroy a building and intimidate a nation.
I suspect that if you had asked any of them on Tuesday morning, "Do you
want to die for freedom and liberty today, or just go about your
business?", they would have chosen the latter.

The American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language, Fourth Edition
defines hero as "A person noted for feats of courage or nobility of
purpose, especially one who has risked or sacrificed his or her life".
Was the mere act of boarding of the doomed flights, or of going to work
in the doomed building, "a feat of courage or nobility of purpose"?  No.

You could just as easily claim that a person who gets killed in an
automobile accident is a hero.  Sorry, just getting killed under
unfortunate circumstances doesn't qualify you as a hero.  If being the
victim of misfortune is considered heroic, the very word "hero" has been
devalued to the point of uselessness.  It is regrettable that the news
media have been misusing the word for years; they are quick to call
anyone in an unusual situation a hero, regardless of their actions or
motives.

The heros in this calamity are the firefighters and rescue workers who
deliberately risked their lives attempting to save others.  Many of them
paid the ultimate price for this, but they did it by choice.  That is
both courageous, and a noble purpose.  That is truly heroic.

There is no dishonor in being the victim of circumstances beyond your
control, but there is no reason to impute unwarranted status to it
either.  We should grieve for the victims and their families.  We should
act to reduce the likelyhood of a repeat of these tragic events.  But we
should call "heros" those who truly do something heroic.

Eric Smith


