>>> that VMS provided - from day one - a lot of code in the RMS
>>> (Record Management Subsystem) for which there is no equivalent in
>>> Unix or TOPS, and which haas immense value for the business
>>
>>Just a minor correction. TOPS-20 did support RMS but not as complete an
>>implementation as in VMS. Don't recall if TOPS-10 ever had RMS support.
>
>It was shipped as an application (kinda like FORTRAN). I finally
>got it bundled in on the CUSP tape.

The version of RMS for TOPS-10 fell behind TOPS-20 towards
the end.  Not sure when but by the time I got there in 1986.
I don't think there was ever good enough documentation to
use from other than COBOL and later FORTRAN.  When the first
version of RMS-10 came out as part of COBOL, my pre DEC
employer tried to get interface information so we could
use RMS instead of trying to build our own indexed file
system and failed.

Unlike VMS where RMS is used by most utilities and user
programs, RMS on TOPS-* was not much used by the supplied
utilities or customer programs outside COBOL shops.

Sam
Last of the RMS-10/-20 maintainers


