>FT nodes using only 1/4 to 1/3 as much hardware (and, presumably, power).
>Given the assembly complexity of triple- and quad-redundant hardware
>designs, it would likely be considerably cheaper as well - as long as the
>up-front design costs could be amortized over sufficient volume.

It might be interesting to bring up IBM's experimental FT/6000 system,
demonstrated at a "Power Academy" exhibit (for ISVs etc.) in January 1996
(in San Francisco).  The computing core (processors and caches up to a
moderately large L3) was triplicated (>200% FT overhead) but main memory
was striped (25% FT overhead), so that the cost overhead for full HW FT
for *large* memory systems would be significantly less (perhaps only 30%)
than fail-over clustering (~100%) -- with no fail-over delays, and full
no-downtime upgradeability.  Software reliability was ensured by having 
a small hypervisor in control of the hardware; this hypervisor was itself
capable of modular no-downtime upgrading.  The operating system (AIX) was
not considered FT (compared to the HW) -- but it had near-instantaneous
checkpoint boot (3 seconds, with a goal of sub-second reboot capability).
The idea was that a reliable application (say, a transaction manager) could
be written to exploit low-latency checkpointing to persistent FT memory, so
that it could restart in seconds from its last checkpoint after a reboot
(possibly triggered by a heartbeat or other performance monitor).  

The project was stopped, unfortunately.  I can imagine no-downtime software
upgrades by building a new checkpoint boot image offline (for a new AIX), and
by maintaining a compatible application checkpoint structure when rolling in
a new version of the primary application.

The hardware was described in:

 M. Abbott et al.  "Durable Memory RS/6000 System Design", Digest of
 the 24th International Symposium on Fault-Tolerant Computing Systems,
 pp 441-423, June 1994.

The software was described in:

 F. Eskesen et al.  "Software Exploitation of a Fault-Tolerant Computer
 with a Large Memory", Digest of the 28th International Symposium on
 Fault-Tolerant Computing Systems, pp 336-345, June 1998.

Michel.


