>hardware failure would be noticed immediately so that the board would be
>shut down (a 'fail-stop' mechanism) before the failure could do any
>externally-visible damage.  ...

This has basically been the approach used in IBM's S/390 (now z-series)
mainframes.  Sometimes microcode can even recover transparently (moving
the last instruction checkpoint to a spare processor sharing the L2 cache,
possible because of the store-through L1); for more severe damage the
operating system's machine-check handler gets involved in recovery.
There were several articles about this about 2 years ago in IBM's Journal
of Research and Development.

