To followup this and some other comments about Stratus, where I've
worked for the last 9 years....  See http://www.stratus.com for the
official marketing blather.

Stratus was founded about 1980 partly on the principle that it was
possible to package standard processors to make highly reliable
fault-tolerant systems.  This was supposed to be cheaper than the
more-custom approach that Tandem took.  Stratus' proprietary operating
system (VOS) has significant Multics heritage, due largely to the
founders' backgrounds.

The early processors were based on Motorola 68K.  IBM sold these as
System/88.

About 1990, the company started working on Intel i860-based systems, and
also started playing in the UNIX arena with an SVR4 port called FTX.

A few years later, the company switched to HP's PA-RISC, and after a few
more years started playing in the HP-UX arena.  Current VOS and UNIX
products are all based on PA8500 and PA8600, though some real fossiles
are available used.  We do have joint marketing/sales/support agreements
with HP for HP-UX systems.

In 1998, Ascend Communications bought Stratus, and a few months later,
Lucent bought Ascend.  In early 1999, between those two events, part of
Stratus was spun off, and is now privately held as Stratus
Technologies.  We have joint engineering agreements with Lucent.  The
company's headquarters is DEC's old world headquarters (*not* the mill)
in Maynard, MA.

Just this past summer, we started shipping Pentium-something-based
"ftServer" systems running Windows2000.  We have joint agreements with
NEC on those.

Current products include the ftServer systems with Windows2000 and
Continuum systems with HP-UX or VOS; we'd probably sell somebody FTX if
they really wanted it.

Over the years, Stratus has absorbed a lot of people from DEC,
Honeywell, and Bull, as well as a fair number with Prime and DG
backgrounds.

Clustering vs hardware-fault-terant systems....  As others pointed out,
Stratus-style systems require reboots for operating system upgrades,
which kills long-term availability numbers.  But there are many shops
that can handle a little scheduled downtime, but can't handle any during
production hours; stock exchanges are in this category (though even
they're going 7x24).  It's possible to design a redundant system to
"unfold" into two separate systems, then upgrade the two (or three)
parts independently; some Stratus hardware has been capable of
unfolding, but in that state it's one non-redundant system.

Traditional Stratus sytems are "Pair & Spare", essentially DMR with
self-checking parts.  CPU boards are self-checked by using two identical
microprocessors on one board, and comparing their outputs
clock-by-clock.  So in practice, there are four physical CPUs for each
logical CPU.  Memories used ECC for checking.  Traditional I/O
processors are lockstepped P&S, but newer ones have a failover
mechanism.  This is still the technology in Continuum systems.

The ftServer systems, on the other hand, are available as real DMR and
TMR, not P&S.  The voters and on-module diagnostics attempt to determine
what broke.

Certainly, as some note, good environment and administration are
critical to availability.  Good hardware and software reduce the risk of
Stupid SysAdmin Tricks, but can't prevent problems.  One thing I hate is
when a customer calls in the morning and says, "Our application started
acting weird last night, so we rebooted the system.  Tell us what
happened."

--Kevin

