Douglas Siebert <dsiebert@excisethis.khamsin.net> wrote:
>
>HP bought Stratus in the mid 90s, they sell fault tolerant products, just
>not under the "HP" name.  If they quit the market, that's news to me.  HP
>even put some lockstep hardware in PA-RISC (similar to what Compaq was
>adding to the 21364) which it was assumed was for the Stratus line, though
>presumably it would be moved to IA-64 eventually.

I don't think that this is quite accurate. On the other hand, everything 
below relies on my notoriously unreliable memory.

Stratus is/was an independent corporation. It was purchased by Alcatel in
1999 or so. They only wanted a bit of the networking portion, and quickly
spun out the computer division of Stratus.

HP does, or did resell Stratus' fault tolerant boxes, which probably
explains the confusion. Stratus had moved from the i860 to the PA-RISC. It
sells their own HP-UX systems, along with their proprietary VOS (which has
some similarities to Multics), FTX, a fault tolerant Unix (well, it used
to sell them; I think FTX is now dead), and lately Windows NT/2000
systems.

They've got some awfully neat technologies. I've admired them from afar
for some time now. See www.stratus.com or comp.sys.stratus for more
information.

