news:3BA399CB.5EF898A@bellatlantic.net...

...

> The reason for the crospost continuation on this thread, imho, is the
> interesting bits in the stratus architecture.  Tandem, dec, IBM, iirc
> pyramid, and many others, have done interesting to me things to
> attempt to achieve fault tolerant systems that really had the goal
> 100% perceived availability and many nines reliability.
>
> If we look at the soft failover things, like clustering (imho)
> offerings, do they really achieve the perception of 100% availblity?

Yes, if a delay of from a few seconds to about a minute (depending on whose
version of clustering you use) when a node fails can be considered to
satisfy that definition (and that delay could be brought down to under a
second without significant change to the technology).

But they don't in general offer the same level of *reliability*, because
they don't guard against undetected hardware faults that allow a node to
continue running but produce incorrect results.  The Stratus and Tandem
approaches, which compare the results generated by replicated hardware
before allowing them to become externally visible, do protect against such
faults.

- bill





