As Lynn just pointed out, there can be an availability impact of *planned*
down-time required for system upgrades with the Stratus/Tandem redundant
approach that can be avoided by a clustering approach if it supports
'rolling upgrades'.  So clustering a pair of such internally-redundant
systems together is required to get the best of all possible worlds with
today's hardware.

It wouldn't seem unreasonable, given the decreasing cost of hardware in
general and the increasing concern with the amount of power it uses, to
consider developing systems that have sufficient internal *checking* to
ensure that they will be 'fail-stop' in nature (i.e., that any fault will be
noticed and result in stopping the system before it becomes externally
visible).  This can be done with minimal additional circuitry compared with
what is required to replicate the entire system and check its outputs
(actually, you need at least three identical functioning systems, since if
two didn't agree you wouldn't know which was wrong; one of Tandem's systems
uses this approach, while Stratus's - and maybe another of Tandem's - uses
quad redundancy because implementing it is simpler).  Parity (or, better,
ECC) memory is an example of this approach used in a single sub-system.

Clustering two such systems together would obtain the benefits of clustered
FT nodes using only 1/4 to 1/3 as much hardware (and, presumably, power).
Given the assembly complexity of triple- and quad-redundant hardware
designs, it would likely be considerably cheaper as well - as long as the
up-front design costs could be amortized over sufficient volume.

