"bad bob" <sfmc68@bellatlantic.net> wrote in message
news:3BA40126.8ABD7D7F@bellatlantic.net...

...

> All of your points are well stated, Yeah clustering provides the
> perception of availablity.  BUT if there is a need for an FT, such as
> tandem, what would be required to do this at the chip level?  The 3
> sysetm approach, as used in the shuttle, as used in an HSC cluster, and
> so on, is a decent approach but has limits.

Triple-modular redundancy ('TMR' - the Tandem 'Integrity' platform approach,
which is operationally similar to the 'pair and spare' quad-redundant
approach used by Stratus) really has little in common with VMS clustering,
HSC or otherwise.

TMR guards against subtle and pernicious (as well as simple) hardware
failure by doing every operation in each of three separate motherboards (or
equivalent), comparing the results, and voting out any single board that
disagrees with the other two.  This gives absolutely non-stop operation
across hardware faults and board replacement (at least those on one of the
boards; interconnect and storage topologies typically use other mechanisms
that may or may not be equally capable of tolerating Byzantine hardware
failures), but doesn't continue through any system upgrade that can't be
applied as a patch to the running system.  In most instances it is also
transparent to application code (i.e., any application gets fault-tolerance
'for free').

('Byzantine' failures technically involve components that deliberately lie,
but I'll use the term here to describe an otherwise-invisible failure that
produces an incorrect result - since in this case they're equivalent.)

VMS-style clustering (and at least to a first approximation other clustering
approaches, including fail-over styles) execute each operation in only one
location.  They are thus susceptible to the kinds of Byzantine hardware
failures that TMR guards against, but *can* provide continuous service
across a full-blown (non-patch) system upgrade as long as the software is
set up to support such a 'rolling upgrade'.  Making a cluster application
'non-stop' requires explicit application code (e.g., with a client/server
application, the clients must be able to switch to a different cluster node
if the one serving them fails, though use of transferrable address alias
mechanisms can make this transparent, and the application must maintain
enough distributed state for this to occur without loss of context).

A cluster of (non-TMR) nodes thus tends to have higher *availability* than a
single TMR node, because the cluster (except for the occasional few-second
fail-over or VMS 'state transition') *never* stops, even for planned
maintenance.  A single TMR node, however, has higher *reliability* than a
cluster of (non-TMR) nodes, because it survives without error those
Byzantine hardware faults that would allow the cluster to produce incorrect
results.

And the two approaches are complementary:  put them together (a cluster of
TMR nodes) and you get even higher availability (the planned downtime
associated with a single TMR node can be avoided, and some multiple failures
are tolerated), with TMR levels of reliability.

What I was suggesting was replacing the TMR approach with board-local (and
chip-local) checks that would provide equivalent guarantees that any
hardware failure would be noticed immediately so that the board would be
shut down (a 'fail-stop' mechanism) before the failure could do any
externally-visible damage.  This would require careful analysis of possible
failures (as contrasted with using the brute-force TMR approach that
determines failure implicitly by a voting process) to ensure that none could
sneak through undetected - but in the process would reduce the three boards
required for TMR (or paired dual boards for pair-and-spare) to a single
board with the detection circuitry on it.  The result (if actually
attainable) would be a system with the reliability of TMR but using only
1/3rd or 1/4th the hardware - but with much less availability because there
is no operational redundancy, just the assurance of fail-stop operation.
However, cluster such systems and you have a platform with the reliability
of TMR and the availability of clustering (which is not that much lower than
the availability of clustered TMR nodes), but using only 1/3rd to 1/4th the
hardware of a real cluster of TMR nodes.

- bill





