"Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> wrote in message
news:9o0n3f$j9d$1@pyrite.mv.net...
> As Lynn just pointed out, there can be an availability impact of *planned*
> down-time required for system upgrades with the Stratus/Tandem redundant
> approach that can be avoided by a clustering approach if it supports
> 'rolling upgrades'.  So clustering a pair of such internally-redundant
> systems together is required to get the best of all possible worlds with
> today's hardware.
>
Actually this is incorrect.  While Stratus has some planned downtime issues,
Tandem does not.

Stratus uses Triple Modular Redundancy(TMR) which is basically 3 seperate
processing elements with a majority rules vote.  This gives you both fault
detection and fault tolerance at a hardware level.

Tandem on the other hand used Dual Modular Redundancy(DMR) which gives you
fault detection at a hardware level.  They then use a fault isolation and
tolerance at a software level to acheive a fully fault tolerant system.  In
effect at the lowest levels, Tandem Himalaya systems are an extremely
tightly integrated cluster of lockstep processor pairs.  On a tandem system,
you can upgrade hardware on the fly.

Clustering only gives you fault tolerance, but does not give you fault
detection.  You need additional hardware to get the detection is you are
going to just use detection.  In the current G series 390 systems this
detection is handled by the cpu effectively having 2 seperate cores in
lockstep.

Tandem has proven to be the #1 system as measures by total uptime(including
both planned and unplanned downtime).  Tandem is also extremely scalable,
quite possible the most scalable system available today.  On the downside,
Tandem does put more responsibility on the software to do the appropriate
actions such at checkpointing the progress.  Tandem does however provide a
lot of software infrastructure to enable this.

Aaron Spink
not speaking for Compaq/HP/Stratus/Intel




