On Fri, 14 Sep 2001 jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:

> In article <Pine.SOL.4.20.0109131041190.18653-100000@garnet.tc.umn.edu>,
>    Phil Mendelsohn <mend0070@tc.umn.edu> wrote:
>
> >I'm really not after causing anyone apoplexy or anything, but I don't mean
> >"at DEC" in the early '70's. Did you work side-by-side, in a direct
> >management role, or directly under him? 
> 
> Neither.  IF he had a tech job to be done he would have
> submitted it to my group.  The people who worked FOR him
> all submitted jobs to my group.  We provided a service to
> the people in his group who needed it.  

What service and what group, if I can ask.

> You won't understand the implications of the following but we also had
> our offices in the same building and drank from the same coffee
> machine.

Um, that's not entirely fair.  I was there myself, though only on one or
two occasions, and I was probably only waist high.  I did witness (and be
mostly bored through) a couple of coffee machine chat sessions.  

BTW, we learned about caffeine withdrawal before my father learned that
setting a least upper bound on cups of coffee drunk from the coffee
machine located immediately outside one's office would prevent having the
DT's late Saturday morning.  Sorry, don't recall which building -- could
find out, but it was Parker St.

Anyway, perhaps I'm only trying to understand what I heard then.

> See above. We provided a technical service.  You won't understand
> because you're too young and not experienced in the ways computing
> was done back then.

I likely will understand, or at least with not much assistance.  What did
you do?  Was it like revision control?  Surely it was more than punch card
or paper tape entry!  Perhaps something more akin to proof-reading?  
Keeping code honest, some sort of pre-test debugging?

> > Maybe that means one can't separate
> >the things I suggest at all, but I do feel that they are fairly confused
> >here.  Of course, I do recognize that they were fairly confused then, too.
> 
> The reason you're confused is because you don't know how work
> was done back then.  

Please note, I didn't ever say *I* was confused.

> People didn't generally type in their programs on-line (there was no
> such thing in Cutler's area...only the PDP-10 provided on-line
> computing service in those days). These people used coding sheets,
> submitted them to us to transform into a specified flavor of bits, and
> went back to their office to write more code on _paper_.

Sorry, madam, but I indeed have experience in how programs were developed
on paper.  I can't say I wrote anything useful, (or user-ful), but
programmed little things in Fortran, *AFTER* doing a flowchart (still have
the stencil) and a coding sheet, used punch card machines (and made
mistakes and deliberately folded, spindled, and mutilated!), and had to
debug a deck of cards that I dropped and shuffled.

> He wasn't; that's why he couldn't.  But, AFAIK, he never got
> himself back into the technical side of the biz..he might have
> when he moved to west coast.

Um, they like to call him System Architect of NT, I believe.  He wrote the
spec, and code, I hear.  I don't blame him for leaving (much), since he
probably saw that DEC wouldn't be developing any more big OS's and built
himself a pretty cushy place to continue to do that.

> > ... I think he would be happier
> >if there were sufficient time for him to just do it himself.  Educated
> >guess.)
> 
> No.  

Assertion?  Source? 

> >> In his case, he dropped bits.  
> >
> >Parity error? <g>  What did he drop?
> 
> 20 of them.

You can't blame Cutler for that.  The pdp-11 was in existence before he
came to DEC from DuPont.  I think you're really mad at Gordon Bell!  So
this starts to sound, again, more like a resentment that makes sense.  
Perhaps something like 

"The -10 never had the address space limitations of the -11, thus the
expenditure of time and money on a kludge of the -11, later known as VAX,
was silly since there already existed a system that could do what they
were trying to do."

But, as I understand it, the market for the -11 in lab and control apps
was bigger than the -10 ever was (that's not what the -10 was for).  You
just would never sell a -10 to a guy who just needed a little box for his
lab, so the -11 *was* justified, at least at the beginning.  I mean, after
all, that's the kind of stuff DEC was founded on.

I won't speak to what the -10's raison d'etre was, but perhaps you would?

> I'm not talking shortcomings, although that's difficult to avoid
> in the case of Cutler.  I'm talking about work done w.r.t. 
> how an operating system got developed.  

This may be more clear when you tell what group you were with and what
service you provided.

-- 
I liked HP before computers, and at one time I liked Compaq,
but I liked DEC better than HP and Compaq put together.




