There was a native version of RMS (RMS-20) which would have allowed a more
head-to-head comparison, at least for applications that used it.  As has
been discussed at length in comp.os.vms, the principal performance advantage
a typical Unix file system has over VMS comes from its different caching
strategies - strategies which are appropriate for a large-memory
environment, as contrasted with those on VMS which were designed for a
tight-memory environment where avoiding the overhead of
application-to-kernel-cache copy activity was also important.

...

> DEC fumbled both the personal computer and workstation markets
> in inter-divisional squabbles.

Maybe.  But major problems included the developing 'industry-standard'
perception of the IBM-PC (making anything other than a pure clone a harder
sell) and DEC's inability to manufacture competing products at comparable
prices.

As far as the PRO was concerned, I don't recall any 'inter-divisional
squabbles' with the Rainbow or the Robin (in fact, there may have been at
least a small amount of synergy due to some shared hardware components).
But there *was* concern in the traditional PDP-11 product lines that the PRO
might cannibalize their higher-priced offerings if it made a complete
RSX-11M-Plus environment too easily accessible - and the result may have
measurably contributed to its demise.

- bill





