Alan Greig wrote:

> port on time (if allowed) and I believe them. It would have helped if
> Compaq hadn't just sold all of its compilers teams (including BLISS
> technology) to Intel as they must work closely on any porting project
> but that's a done deal now.

Well, not all the compiler folks and technology have ended up (or will 
end up) at Intel.


> Porting from Alpha to IA64 shouldn't require anything like the effort
> needed for the VAX to Alpha port.  You might be amused to know that
> much of VMS is still written in Macro-32 which then is cross-compiled
> to Alpha and soon IA64..

Well, technically it isn't 'cross-compiled'.  It is natively compiled. 
The AMACRO compiler runs on an OpenVMS Alpha system, reading Macro-32 
source code (plus some extensions), and generates OpenVMS Alpha object 
files.  A 'cross-compiler' (at least to me) runs on a different system 
than it generates code for.

The moving of Macro-32 code from OpenVMS Alpha to IA64 should be easier 
in some regards (you already had to decorate your code with .CALL_ENTRY 
and .JSB_ENTRY directives for instance when moving from VAX to Alpha), 
but harder in other ways.  How much Macro-32 code writes into R0 for 
instance? (and for those you haven't read their IA64 architecture 
guides, R0 is a readonly register fixed as 0 much like R31 on the Alpha).

-- 
John Reagan
Compaq Pascal/{A|I}MACRO Project Leader



