Alan,
Your quotes from Winkler's January speech at Compaq's annual
meeting with stock analysts show that Winkler and Capellas
had already determined by January that NSK Himalaya's port
to Alpha was to be ended and replaced by a port to IPF.
(5 months before they bothered to tell anyone in the porting
team or any of its mgmt chain.)  That change in target would
only make business sense if they had already decided they
would not be funding at least 10 more years of Alpha chip
development.  (2 years to complete & ship the NSK port, plus
at least 8 years to amortize NSK customers' migration efforts.)
I.e. no more Alpha chips after EV9, if not earlier.   Winkler
apparently was confident enough about this decision being
a done deal that he (totally unnecessarily) mentioned the
NSK aspect of it to analysts at that January meeting.

I think they expected back then to be announcing a broad deal
with Intel within a month or two.  This was 5 months before
Fiorina and Capellas began discussions of adding a rebranded
HP-UX IPF machine to Compaq's server product portfolio for
more complete coverage in the commercial datacenter market.

The Intel deal took many more months to finalize than Compaq
initially expected.  At Intel lawyers' insistence, they decided
to go stealthy until the deal was fully detailed and signed.
Luckily, they were able to stuff most of the cat back into the bag.
None of the analysts understood the import of Winkler's January
NSK-->IPF statement.  You (Alan) understood it and reported
it on comp.os.vms, but this merely added a little fuel to an
already noisy discussion of VMS futures. Folks in NSK
development missed that statement entirely. It appears that
Shannon did not catch on or believe the implications.
It appears that no large Alpha customers took notice then.

The timeline is inconsistent with Fiorina forcing a reluctant
Compaq to jump off the Digital Alpha boat.  Compaq was
already eagerly jumping.  At most, Fiorina didn't try to push
Compaq back onto an Alpha-forever plan, and did invite
a now ring-free Compaq to sit with HP at the big end of
the stately IPF boat.

Winkler's gaffe in pre-announcing the NSK redirection
could now be a good thing, serving as public proof that the
eventual demise of Alpha was already certain before the
HP dalliance began, even if we all didn't believe it yet.

 ---  Duane Sand, not speaking for Compaq.
















