Jan Vorbrueggen wrote:
> Yup, you are missing a lot. For one, taking a message apart requires that you
> know a) the relative endianess of the participant systems and b) the exact
> data types of all components of the message. Not trivial, and not very
> performant in a cluster even if it does work.

Consider all of the TCPIP applications, including NFS. They seem to have no
problem with endianness because a standard was set (big endian) and anyone
wanting to participate in that application makes sure that they treat the
binary data  properly. So the endianness of the file system becomes irrelevant
when you use stuff such as NFS.

(of course, the contents of the files you access is another story).

Consider file formats such as TIFF which made allowances for both endiannes to
be usable. (there is a sequence at the top of the file which defines if the
file is big or little endian (motorola or intel).

On the other hand, many other file formats (flash .swf files for instance)
assume a specific endianness which wasn't even documented in the first sdk
that was released years ago.

It really depends on what HP intends to do with Tru64. I think that the
logical thing is to leave Tru64 alone on Alpha and just bring to HP-UX the
stuff that Tru64 has but HP-UX doesn't. If you're going to migrate to another
platform, might as well do it only once.

If they get Tru64 folks to first migrate to IA64, and then to HP-UX, they
won't be too happy.

This is why I feel that the HP and Compaq funeral announcement is good news
for EV7 because I think that HP will keep Tru64 on Alpha while HP-UP is
upgraded on IA64 to allow painless migration from Tru64-Alpha to HP-UX-IA64.


