Emulating the parity bit in software is IMHO not a good idea. What we
did when running into this problem is:

Since it's save to assume that forced wrong parity writes are the only
source of parity errors in the emulator, it's enough to have a flag
indicating the existence of "bad" spots in emulated memory and eg. a
list of these spots. If the above flag is set, access to (physical)
memory is redirected to a check-routine which compares the address to
the bad spots. If a bad spot is hit then mcheck occurs, else normal.

cheers


