> Isn't Agilent the one with all the engineering brains to build high quality
> instruments ? (Is it agilent that now builds those reverse polish calculators
> ? (do they still make them ?).

  I really don't know. But high-end instruments and calculators are not really
what I was talking about. I meant more on the order of experience with
engineering computer systems. At least above the level of "buy a cpu from A,
mother board from B, disk from C, OS from Microsoft, and put it allllll together"
engineering. More along the lines of "The Crossbar for our Superdome system uses
a 400Mhz nonblocking protocol and is implemented in a six-layer copper, 18
million transistor custom IC"... which was developed inhouse. Not exactly the
same as choosing case colors.

>
>
> Seems to me that what is left of HP is more or less a copycat image of Compaq:
> primarily a wintel box maker with a succesful printer division and an attempt
> at keeping what is left of their original enterprise business.

  HP has experience with large and involved engineering projects. It's management
grew up in a company which was predicated more on engineering than marketing.
Sort of makes me think of Digital with an actual marketing department. Compaq was
little more than an assembly and marketing arm of Intel & Microsoft. They didn't
grow up with engineers who would walk into a manager's office and say "We need at
least five months exclusive access to twenty million dollars of computing
hardware to verify the cache coherency protocol across the crossbar for our xyz
project" without blinking. Or needing eight meetings just to get a vague idea of
what the engineer was asking for, and then five more to turn them down.

  They also have a marketing division which knows how to sell something other
than pretty boxes. And a sales force which already sells large systems, and
doesn't have to go from selling <$1,000 boxes to >$1,000,000 systems on the
say-so of a memo from on high.

  IMHO, the problems Alpha & VMS have been experiencing were not technical
problems as much as they were managerial, marketing and sales problems. I see HP
as correcting these problems fairly effectively. How much they can salvage from
the Compaq-Digital trainwreck, and how much they *will* salvage remains to be
seen.

--Carl



