Sept. 8, 2001, 9:12AM
Merger a bad deal for all concerned
By DWIGHT SILVERMAN
Copyright 2001 Houston Chronicle

MAYBE the streak of light East Coast residents saw this week wasn't a
Russian rocket re-entering the atmosphere. Perhaps it was the start of
the Compaq Computer Corp. and Hewlett-Packard Co. merger flaming out.

If there was ever a bad idea turned into an awful deal, this is it. Here
are two companies struggling in a depressed technology market, both
seemingly lost in an increasingly stark wilderness. Neither company is
healthy enough to swallow the other.

Want another analogy? Try this: If you've got someone in danger of
drowning, you don't dispatch another non-swimmer to the rescue.

When you talk to employees at the Houston campus of Compaq about the
planned acquisition by Hewlett-Packard, the word "shocked" always seems
to come up. But there's also a sense of sadness and defeat.

Compaq's corporate culture thrived on competition. After all, this is a
company born of a master plan to challenge the mighty IBM. The notion
that Compaq must roll over and show its underbelly to rival
Hewlett-Packard is almost too much to bear.

The brain drain has already begun. R�sum�s are being prepped. There are
sardonic jokes about the plunging value of stock options being offset by
selling Compaq polo shirts on eBay.

Some employees are asking: If the game is over, why keep playing?

[Deal may be dead already]

Talking to shareholders, industry observers and employees, I believe a
lot of them think the deal may be stillborn.

The stock market has expressed disdain for the merger. Both Compaq's and
Hewlett-Packard's shares have done swan dives this week. The premium
that Compaq shareholders would have received in Hewlett-Packard stock
has been virtually wiped out. The majority of equity analysts quoted in
the press have indicated they don't like the deal, although industry
analysts have been more intrigued.

Then there's the aura that now surrounds the two companies' chief
executives, Hewlett-Packard's Carly Fiorina and Compaq's Michael D.
Capellas. Once high-flying darlings, they're now perceived as desperate
CEOs looking for solid ground in the quicksand of an information
technology industry in recession.

In addition, Capellas -- whose energy and image as a tech geek once
provided a much-needed morale boost at Compaq -- is now being referred
to internally as "the one who sold us out."

Over the last few days, Capellas, Fiorina and their lieutenants have
donned game faces and tried hard to convince various constituents that,
hey, no kidding, this really is a good deal. They point to complementary
strengths, such as Hewlett-Packard's Unix servers and Compaq's
Intel/Windows-based models.

But there's also plenty of overlap that will have to be adjusted,
particularly in consumer products, handhelds and business desktops.

That "adjustment" will include 15,000 layoffs, most of them likely to
come from the already shell-shocked ranks at Compaq. Those employees
will be tossed into a growing pool of tech workers already unable to
find jobs in a shrinking industry.

[Sales will be difficult]

While the deal is being finalized, Compaq still has to sell its goods
and services. But how many people are going to want to buy high-tech
products from a company that's going away? That's particularly true of
consumers, for whom buying a PC is painful enough when the manufacturer
is a healthy one.

Folks in Houston are rightly concerned about the local impact. Although
Capellas has said Hewlett-Packard will maintain a "significant presence"
here, history has shown that presence is likely to atrophy.

You need only look at what happened in Massachusetts after Compaq bought
Digital Equipment Corp. Compaq executives also promised a "significant
presence" in Digital's home base. But there are now just 5,000 Compaq
employees in Massachusetts, compared with 8,000 when the Digital
acquisition closed.

There's no reason to think it will be any different here in Houston,
with one possible exception. There's an off chance Houston could become
a haven for beleaguered Palo Alto refugees.

This is a bad deal all around, and hopefully market forces, regulators
or horrified shareholders will shut it down if the good sense of its top
executives does not.



