HP CEO Fiorina pitching the deal of her career

PALO ALTO, Calif., Sept. 7 � Hailed as the consummate saleswoman,
Hewlett-Packard Co.(NYSE:HWP) Chief Executive Carly Fiorina must close
the biggest deal of her career in coming days by convincing skeptical
investors that her company and Compaq Computer Corp.(NYSE:CPQ) are
better off merged than separate.

       Fiorina, tapped to become CEO of the merged HP, wrapped up a
whirlwind campaign this week that included hastily scheduled appearances
at conferences sponsored by Salomon Smith Barney and SG Cowen, a meeting
with institutional investors in New York on Thursday and a management
dinner in Houston that night.

       Back in San Francisco and draped in power red, Fiorina on Friday
told Reuters that the shareholder and analyst meetings were going "very
well."

       "The more people listen, the more they understand. The more they
understand, the more they like it," she said, referring to the HP-Compaq
merger proposal that was made public in a surprise announcement on
Monday and prompted investors to send shares of the betrothed companies
to five-year lows.

       During her speech at a San Francisco conference commemorating the
fiftieth anniversary of the U.S.-Japan peace treaty, Fiorina showed no
signs of wavering under scrutiny.

       "This week's announcement is about catalyzing change across an
industry," she said. "Once you embark on the change journey there is no
looking back."

       Wall Street, however, could not move beyond its objections and
remained out of step with the CEOs that forged the deal that would
create a technology giant to rival market titan International Business
Machines Corp.(NYSE:IBM)

       "It's a curious merger that's going to take a while to sell,"
said John Forelli, portfolio manager at Independence Investment LLC.
STOCKS BOUNCE OFF BOTTOM The severity of investor reaction came as
something of a surprise to JP Morgan Research Analyst Daniel Kunstler.

       "While a lot of us in the investment community had clear doubts
about the deal and objections to it, I don't think that any of us
foresaw that it would be as brutal as it has been," he said.

       HP and Compaq shares inched higher on Friday as bargain hunters
tiptoed in, but it was not enough to undo the damage. HP closed Friday
at $18.08, down 22 percent on the week, while Compaq wrapped up the week
14 percent lower at $10.59.

       The resulting value of the deal -- in which Compaq shareholders
will get 0.6325 shares of HP for each share of Compaq stock -- remained
at about $19.3 billion compared with its original price tag of nearly
$25 billion.

       By and large, analysts and fund managers agree that the merger
will help the companies cut costs, but they are less sure that bigger
will equate with better in this case.

       "You usually like one plus one to equal two," Kunstler said.
"There is a certain amount of apprehension (with this deal) that one
plus one is going to be significantly less than two."

       Critics of the proposed corporate marriage wonder why HP -- which
made an unsuccessful pitch to expand its services business by pairing up
with Price Waterhouse Coopers -- now wants to wed Compaq, become the
world's largest computer maker and get further entrenched in that
segment's brutal price war.

       They fear that the merger threatens to dilute HP's flagship
high-margin printer business.

       They also worry that the companies will lose customers to
aggressive competitors as they focus on combining operations.

       And, considering that most hardware mergers turn out to be less
than successful, they also are dumbfounded as to why two companies that
haven't had stellar acquisition records are pairing up in the darkest
days the tech sector has ever seen.

DISSENTERS HAVE DEFECTED, BOARDS ARE ON BOARD Fiorina, the first female
CEO of a top 20 U.S. company, is in her element when she's making a
pitch.

       She climbed the sales ranks at AT&T (NYSE:T) and helped drive the
highly lucrative initial public offering of Lucent Technologies
(NYSE:LU) before taking on the daunting task of reinvigorating Palo
Alto, California-based HP two years ago.

       Since then, the ground under Fiorina has shifted and she also has
been forced to grapple with the operational challenges served up by a
technology spending downturn that has hobbled
many other Silicon Valley stalwarts. Despite the market's hand-wringing,
Fiorina may already have much of the support she needs to pull off the
merger.

       That's partly because dissenters tend to be sellers, said Mike
Holland, founder of the Holland Balance Fund, who bets that remaining
shareholders will approve the deal.
       There's also a hefty $675 million breakup fee attached to the
agreement that is likely to keep other suitors out of the game, analysts
said.
       Sources close to the companies said that both boards remain
committed to seeing the deal through, despite market backlash.
       One of those people, who requested anonymity, said the companies
were taking a "hell or high water" mentality while they try and convince
investors of the substantial cost and revenue synergies that can be
obtained by the combination.
       In addition to the revenue gains that company officials project
from combining operations like Hewlett-Packard's printer business with
both firms' PC operations, executives believe that Compaq CEO Capellas
has what it takes to wring out the deal's expected $2.5 billion in cost
savings.
       "If people want to vote against the deal, they sell the stock,"
said Holland, who noted that sales pitches will not change minds in the
way that delivering quarter after quarter of good financial results
will.
       "This is easily the most important thing she has ever done," said
Holland, who sold positions in HP and Compaq before Monday's surprise
announcement of the merger plan.



