Compaq customers concerned
Worries over service become an issue
By GREG HASSELL
Copyright 2001 Houston Chronicle

LAST week, Dee Griffin made up her mind.

She was definitely going to buy a new Compaq computer, choosing the
company's handheld iPaq over an army of high-tech alternatives.

"It's new, and it's fresh," said Griffin, a TV news anchor in the
Midwest. "I like what it's able to do."

Then she picked up the paper on Tuesday and saw that Compaq is being
gobbled up by Hewlett-Packard Co. in a deal initially valued at $25
billion. Suddenly, her ardor for the iPaq went ice cold.

"I asked myself, what will happen if I buy this computer?" Griffin said.
"If I buy it, will the service remain up to standard?

"It's an unknown, and the unknown scares me. I decided I didn't want to
get caught up in the hassles at this stage in the game."

As Compaq and Hewlett-Packard try to hustle to the altar, Houston's
home-grown technology giant has more to worry about than the plunging
stock prices that have slashed the deal's value by $5 billion. Compaq
has to figure out a way to keep its brand alive and capable of
commanding consumer loyalty -- at least until the merger is completed.

The danger is that skittish consumers could cancel Compaq purchases and
spend their cash with rivals like Dell or IBM.

"That's going to be a big problem," said Rob Enderle, a technology
analyst with the Giga Information Group. "There is even a chance Compaq
sales will crater in the fourth quarter, particularly its retail
segment. This is a time that Compaq must be seen as alive and vibrant,
but every competitor on the planet will portray them as dead and
buried."

"It is a very dangerous time for Compaq if they don't maneuver carefully
and effectively during this period," agreed Tim Bajarin, an industry
consultant and president of Creative Strategies.

Put yourself in the place of a typical computer buyer, Bajarin said. The
No. 1 thing that you want to make certain of is the computer maker you
choose will be there tomorrow, willing and able to offer good service
for your machine.

"If you look at Compaq's advertising in the past three years, in almost
every one you will see a strong service-and-support message," Bajarin
said. "Compaq has a lot of selling to do."

To be sure, Compaq and Hewlett-Packard are not going into this image
battle without some powerful weapons at their disposal.

"These are two of the most respected manufacturers in the industry. They
are trusted by consumers," said Alex Kalamarides, a senior vice
president of research at AMI-Partners. "That gives them a little
latitude, marketing- and branding-wise."

And yet, even those who believe that the prognostications of doom are
greatly exaggerated will concede that Compaq must calm consumers
quickly.

"We recognize there is going to be some concern among customers," one
Compaq executive said. "If they are weighing a decision about buying a
Compaq product or a competitors', the uncertainty could sway them to buy
a computer by another manufacturer. We need to assure them that the
customer support will still be there.

"We have a strong track record for the quality of our products, and that
won't go away. All customer commitments made by Compaq -- that includes
contracts and product and service warranties -- will be honored
according to the terms both prior to and subsequent to the completion of
the merger with the Hewlett-Packard Co."

Compaq is still formulating a plan to get that message out.

"People in advertising and marketing were taken just as much by surprise
as the general public," the Compaq executive added. "It takes some time
for these executives to huddle up and decide what the best strategy will
be."

At this point, Compaq is not convinced it must vastly raise its
marketing and advertising spending to calm consumers.

"It's something we will look at, but it's not something at this time we
have made a final decision on," the executive said.

Analyst Enderle thinks Compaq could be playing with fire unless it
launches a media and public relations blitz that goes well beyond
business as usual. Unfortunately, few high-tech companies outside of
Apple have consistently proven themselves to be adroit advertisers, he
said.

"Compaq has to create a powerful campaign. This requires a substantial
amount of creativity," Enderle said. "This is when a marketing
organization earns its stripes."

RadioShack, a major retail partner of Compaq's, is doing its part by
teaching sales staff how to respond to questions about Compaq's
uncertain future. To date, RadioShack sales personnel are not getting
peppered with a lot of worried questions about Compaq.

"For a lot of consumers, this deal hasn't sunk in yet. So it's not a
major issue, in my opinion," RadioShack Corp. Chairman Leonard Roberts
said. "But it will be a major issue if it continues to be a news story,
if the deal doesn't go smoothly with investors, regulators, things like
that. If, in a year from now, these companies aren't merged as planned,
we'll have an issue."

So far, a lot of the headlines about the merger have been negative,
especially the ones about investors dumping Compaq and Hewlett-Packard
shares. It's clear the atmosphere is too turbulent for Griffin, who is
still holding off on that iPaq purchase. But she says she can be won
over by Compaq, if the company pushes the right marketing buttons in the
weeks and months to come.

"They need to tell me how all this will affect the consumer," she said.
"Spell it out for the average Joe -- or Jane."

