It's interesting, disheartening and more than a bit alarming that a
citizen who expresses a desire for his or her country to govern its
own affairs is labeled as a bigot.  Is the U.S. the only country in
the world?  Of course not.  But just as your house is not the only one
in the neighborhood, you wouldn't want the raising of your kids, your
financial statements or your interpersonal relationships to be
scrutinized by the people next door or the family around the corner,
nor would you desire their approval or heed their disapproval.

And why would the U.S. want to take cues from other nations around the
world anyway?  England continues to finance the world's largest
welfare recipients (a.k.a. the royal family), the government of France
has imposed hiring/firing criteria that's bringing the country to its
knees, and nations that have implemented socialized medicine have
people waiting in line for the simplest of services.  So I ask again,
why would the U.S. want to model itself, even in the slightest, after
other nations who are apparently incapable of properly taking care of
themselves?

You don't have to be a betting man to like the odds that Paddy O'Brien
thinks the U.N. is a benevolent organization.  Unfortunately, there's
a high number of U.S. citizens who think the same way, brought upon by
an education system more interested with indoctrination than with
teaching kids to read and write, and a curriculum chock full of
propaganda such as corporations are always as evil, CEOs are always
heartless, and every citizen is a victim of evil corporations and
heartless CEOs, and is therefore unjustifiably downtrodden.

Now, before anyone intends to rant about how the U.S. keeps sticking
its nose into the affairs of other countries, don't bother because I
wish the U.S. would cease its meddling as well.  However, the two
political parties we have in power at the moment believe it's their
duty to force everyone, other countries and its own citizens and
businesses included, to act in a manner that meets their approval.
This, by the way, is what started this discussion in the first place
.. the fact that the Democrats and Republicans will use any
strong-arm tactic necessary to feed their hunger for power, and they
don't care how many dead bodies are left in their wake.


