Kilgallen@SpamCop.net (Larry Kilgallen) writes:

> Later VAXes also have a penalty for unaligned access, but it is hidden
> in the hardware.

There was a paper in the Digital Technical Journal on the decoder of the NVAX
(or NVAX+) chip - the one that was built in the same process as the 21064. 
Basically, a RISC subset of the VAX ISA is hard-coded and fast, and assumes
things such as aligned accesses (and page faults not occuring, probably). 
Everything else is handled by microcode and is substantially slower. The later
S/370 et al. systems do similar things. 

	Jan


