Bzzzzttttt!  Cluelessness-Alert!  Terry of late doesn't seem to remember to
engage his brain before releasing the brake.

First, Compaq proved with Win2K and Win64 on Alpha that simply having
systems up and running was no guarantee that they would ship.  Strike One.

Second, Tru64 and HP-UX converge only with *major* pain to at least one
party.  HP-UX is a big-endian system, whereas Tru64 is a little-endian
system:  even after maximal efforts by the vendor to ease the process,
application binaries don't port across at all, *source code* doesn't port
across without examination and usually modification, neither do *existing
(non-text) files* ...  Strike Two.

Third, HP-UX *already has* clustering facilities (no, I don't maintain
they're equal to Tru64's, but they're there), and Tru64 *doesn't* yet have
the FT capabilities Terry has lately been hawking.  Just as with most other
parts of the merger, there's a lot more duplication than synergy here -
which means that the likely result is elimination of the duplication.
Strike Three.


Time is running out, folks:  you may have become used to the Death of a
Thousand Cuts over the past seven years or so, but this summer Compaq tossed
away the scalpel and brought out the cleaver.  There's about one shot left
with any chance at all to save the software (and maybe even the hardware)
that you seem to value:  piss away this opportunity by hoping for the best,
and it's gone forever.

