"Terry C. Shannon" wrote:
> 
> EV7 will ship. Pass One booted VMS and T64 early last month. How this will
> be rationalized with HP remains to be seen, but when you converge T64's
> clustering and FT and management capabilities (oh, alomost forgot SSI) and
> HP-UX's vast installed base, you have something that ought to put the fear
> of God into Mister Solaris hisself!

In order to acheive this HP needs to overcome a number of technical 
hurdles.

1.	Endianess
2.	Big code merge
3.	Preservation of one of the API's at least HP-UX or Tru64

In addition there are a number of organisational and resource 
hurdles.

1.	The winner (HP-UX) takes the spoils.
2.	HP-UX is currently transitioning from HP-PA -> IA64 does
	the HP-UX team have the resources or the will to participate
	in a train smash merge at the same time.
3.	Tru64 is currently transitioning from Alpha -> IA64 does
	the Tru64 team have the resources to participate in a
	train smash merge at the same time.
4.	This will all be conducted against a background of declining 
	sales and staff cuts just to add a bit of additional
	stress.
5.	HP-UX has a much larger ISV portfolio than Tru64 and HP 
	will not want to do anything that will compromise this,
	they have had enough difficulty transitioning from HP-UX
	10.x to HP-UX 11 with their ISV's.

They also need to retain their existing customer base, this may 
be harder than it will be for the Wintel folks. The fact that the 
Wintel boxes are commodity servers means that customers are less 
concerned about the long term prospects of either vendors, they 
are buying commodity products with a relatively short life span
and if either vendor messes up they can go to one of the other 
Wintel vendors quite easily.

On the other hand the enterprise customers buying Tru64/HP-UX 
are much more concerned about the long term, will my platform 
be available/supported/viable in 5-6-7 years time is a serious
question in this space but hardly relevant to the Wintel 
space.

So HP will be very cautious about any changes to their premier
UNIX platform (HP-UX) that may cause customers to have concerns
about their investement.

Almost all the analysts agree that this merger offers Sun and 
IBM the opportunity to grow market share at the expense of
Compaq/HP and this is assuming that HP does a good job.

regards
Andrew Harrison
