>May I humbly ask, what you use as Word, Excel and Powerpoint alternatives?

  My favored open-source Word alternative is Maxwell, but I know that 
others prefer ABIWord or LYx.  There's also the WYSIWYG word processor 
that comes with StarOffice, which is not open-source, but still an 
alternative for Linux (and I think Solaris).

  For a spreadsheet I use GNOME's Gnumeric.

  Talk rolls around now and then about writing a more "bona fide" 
alternative to PowerPoint, but ultimately we end up using a combo of 
animated GIFs and auto-refreshing HTML, and use the browser as its 
viewer.  If anyone ever got serious about it, they'd probably just 
write a simple WYSIWYG application which spits out animated GIFs and 
auto-refreshing HTML.  :-)

>I know of the (said to be very good) XESS spreadsheet (http://www.ais.com)
>but it is not free. And _I_ still don't know any freeware reading PPT files.

  Data file level compatability with products that use proprietary 
formats is a problem, yes .. there are a few open-source applications 
which (eg) read/write older Word formats, but Microsoft likes to 
change their formats too fast to make keeping up worthwhile.  It is 
ultimately better to standardize one's office infrastructure on more 
standardized and documented protocols (html, pdf, et al).

>And the fact, that almost every PC bought in a store comes preinstalled
>with M$ and you have virtually no chance to get the box without the s/w.

  This is a function of MS's licensing.  If the OEM's only paid for 
copies of Windows that they preloaded on computers, then unless they 
sold *mostly* non-Windows systems they would end up paying more than 
if they simply paid by the system.  Shipping the PC with Windows does 
not create a pressure to use it, though, nor does it prevent the OEM 
from preloading a boot manager which allows the user to select among 
multiple preloaded operating systems.  OEM's could fairly painlessly 
preload a linux distro *and* FreeBSD, each in their own 2GB disk 
partitions, without taking up a too-significant fraction of the huge 
cheap hard disks PC's are shipping with these days (last I looked, 
it's not economically sensical to get an EIDE smaller than 20GB, and 
40GB's are also pretty painless).  If the user didn't mind FAT, the 
Linux or FreeBSD systems could mount "C:" as /hda3 and use a symlink 
into it for /home.

