>> >> But maybe at some future time, HP/UX is equivalent to Tru64, the next
>> >> boxes you buy to plug into your Tru64 cluster are HP/UX boxes and you
>> >> go from there.
>> >
>> > Perhaps you'd like to discuss that with cluster engineering.  When they
> were
>> > talking about integrating Itanic hardware into existing clusters, they
> were
>> > talking about using said hardware in little-endian fashion.  Integrating
> a
>> > big-endian system like HP/UX into a little-endian cluster like Tru64 is
> an
>> > entirely different ball of wax.
>> >
>>
>> Right.  But message passing is still message passing or am I missing
>> something?
> 
> Just a bit.
> 
> Let's start with a shared file system.  Vanilla Unix-style text files are in
> fact easy to share across mixed-endian environments, but step outside text
> and you fall off a cliff, and there's *no* good way to work around it
> (except to share only byte-string data, including the meta-data in the file
> system itself).  Mixed-endian NFS environments just punt the issue by
> leaving the interpretation of in-file data up to the application, and can
> more or less get away with that because they're relatively loosely-coupled,
> use XDR mechanisms to handle endian-sensitive data in messages, and don't
> support concurrent shared access to storage devices (hence at least avoid
> the on-disk meta-data problem).  But start using multiple instances of a
> single application - some big-endian and others little-endian - that share
> the same data and if said shared data is anything but text-style
> (byte-string) in nature the application code must deal explicitly with
> translation issues, because *nothing* else can.
> 
> Now, can you really say you've got an integrated cluster if an application
> group can only run on a same-endian subset of nodes within it?
> 
> Then, of course, there's the cluster infrastructure itself.  It's certainly
> possible to use something equivalent to XDR to allow nodes to make sense of
> messages from different-endian compatriots, but they still have to use
> common message protocols and internal cluster cooperation mechanisms.
> 
> So to mix HP-UX and Tru64 nodes in a single cluster, they must use the same
> cluster protocols - which means that one of them has to adopt the other's
> cluster architecture right down to its roots (and throw out its own).
>
> Otherwise, they may be able to talk to each other (message-passing is indeed
> message-passing if viewed at a sufficiently low level) but won't be able to
> understand a word.  Though this would be a major effort, at least in this
> case a solution is possible.
> 
> But if you want a *really* integrated cluster (including Terry's
> currently-favored 'single system image' features), then both systems need to
> use the same configuration files in order to present a unified management
> interface (it's not the Unix way to glue on some upper management layer that
> translates between different configuration data flavors:  Unix people change
> configurations with editors, not 'management utilities').  This requires
> convergence-tweaking throughout the system.
> 
> I'm sure I've only scratched the surface above, but perhaps it will at least
> begin to explain why mixing different hardware (Alpha and Itanic) in a
> cluster whose nodes are all running Tru64 in little-endian mode is at least
> an order of magnitude less work than mixing little-endian Tru64 systems with
> big-endian HP-UX systems in a cluster.
> 

	Look's like a substantial engineering challenge.  Thanks for the
	overview.

				Rob



