What needs to be defined, evidently, is the purpose of anti-trust
legislation.  Its intent is to protect CONSUMERS, not competitors,
from a monopoly.  The parade of "witnesses" in the Microsoft case had
very few (if any) dissatisfied customers.  Instead, we had competitor
after competitor complaining that Microsoft acted in an unfair manner.
An intensive and successful marketting campaign should not be confused
with monopolistic practices.

And what also needs to pointed out is that businesses and individuals
who put all their eggs into the Microsoft basket have to suffer the
consequences of their actions.  I've never heard of an instance where
a prospective computer purchaser was coerced into buying an MS-based
PC at gunpoint.  They made their decisions on their own, and if
political bodies think that consumers are too stupid to know
otherwise, then we should question the political bodies and not the
consumers.

At the risk of insulting the Europeans who pay attention to this
group, it's irrelevant what Europeans think of American law, whether
we're talking about software or gun control or anything else.  Their
opinion should carry no weight in this or any other discussion since
we are not obligated to acquiesce to their policies (nor should we
ever be).  Who cares if there are European "standards" for what is
considered a monoply ... reality is a better yardstick than the
whimsical notions of socialist-infiltrated governments.

Keep in mind, I'm a much bigger fan of VMS than Microsoft (as evident
by the fact that I pay attention to this group), and it pains me to
see VMS slipping into obscurity.  However, we should not direct our
anger at a company who marketted their products better than DEC/Compaq
did (and it's doubtful HP will do any better).  Just because MS beat
out VMS (and others) in the marketplace does not mean government
intervention is required.


