On Sat, 08 Sep 2001 08:19:35 GMT, politics2000@hotmail.com <politics2000@hotmail.com> wrote:
> What needs to be defined, evidently, is the purpose of anti-trust
> legislation.  Its intent is to protect CONSUMERS, not competitors,
> from a monopoly.  The parade of "witnesses" in the Microsoft case had
> very few (if any) dissatisfied customers.  Instead, we had competitor
> after competitor complaining that Microsoft acted in an unfair manner.
> An intensive and successful marketting campaign should not be confused
> with monopolistic practices.

I agree that the legislation is intended to protect consumers, however
that has no bearing on how a prosecution is conducted. Anti-trust
legislation proscribes certain _behaviour_ and proving a company has
behaved in a certain way need not involve it's customers in any way -
particularly if you've got some juicy internal memos ;-). To paraphrase
you: A weak and pitiful prosecution should not be confused with innocence.

> And what also needs to pointed out is that businesses and individuals
> who put all their eggs into the Microsoft basket have to suffer the
> consequences of their actions.  I've never heard of an instance where
> a prospective computer purchaser was coerced into buying an MS-based
> PC at gunpoint.  They made their decisions on their own, and if
> political bodies think that consumers are too stupid to know
> otherwise, then we should question the political bodies and not the
> consumers.

Well, if you think that control can only be exerted at gunpoint, then
you're woefully ignorant. Microsoft has constructed all sorts of 
barriers to entry into it's market - enough to give it an _unfair_
advantage in that market and that is illegal - in America and in Europe.
Why is it illegal? It's illegal in order to protect consumers, because
it is in the consumers best interest to have some competition in a
marketplace. The anti-trust legislation doesn't legislate for competition
- impossible unless the government got into the business of starting
competing companies. So, it does the next best thing. It legislates for a
fair market, so if a competing company starts up it can't be stifled by
monopoly power.

> At the risk of insulting the Europeans who pay attention to this
> group, it's irrelevant what Europeans think of American law, whether
> we're talking about software or gun control or anything else.  Their
> opinion should carry no weight in this or any other discussion since
> we are not obligated to acquiesce to their policies (nor should we
> ever be).  Who cares if there are European "standards" for what is
> considered a monoply ... reality is a better yardstick than the
> whimsical notions of socialist-infiltrated governments.

Oh please... where do I begin with this? First, at no point during this
discussion have I, or anyone else AFAIR, made an attack on American
politics - I could, quite easily, but I didn't. Second, we've had to put up
with rants from you and TTK Ciar about gun control, as if it had anything
to do with the issue. Third, American anti-trust law is quite strong enough
to deal with abuse of monopoly power - and some of it has been around since
the 1890 and has been used against the likes of Standard Oil, American
Tobacco Company and AT&T. This is not a new issue in the US and it has
_nothing_ to do with European standards. Fourth, if we're talking about
what laws should be (which we weren't but never mind) then we're engaging
in a _moral_ debate, which should not respect any national boundaries.

Fifth and final point, which has essentially nothing to do with this
discussion, but nevertheless... I take exception to your statement that
European opinion should carry no weight within the US. This is patently
false. If we were to accept that argument then US opinion should carry
no weight in China, Kosovo or Afghanistan. It is a statement of US
foreign policy that it's voice and, in the case of Kosovo, the boot prints
of it's soldiers should be felt in these countries. Like it our not, you
need interact with the rest of the world and part of that interaction is
necessarily involves some sort of give and take. In the case of the EU
and the US this is normally conducted in a reasonably civilised manner, 
between diplomats. In the case of, say, the US and Panama it has been
conducted with soldiers. The idea that the opinions of one country have
no bearing on others is facile. My apologies to other readers for
bringing this _extremely_ off topic paragraph in, but I just couldn't
let this stand.

> Keep in mind, I'm a much bigger fan of VMS than Microsoft (as evident
> by the fact that I pay attention to this group), and it pains me to
> see VMS slipping into obscurity.  However, we should not direct our
> anger at a company who marketted their products better than DEC/Compaq
> did (and it's doubtful HP will do any better).  Just because MS beat
> out VMS (and others) in the marketplace does not mean government
> intervention is required.

The important word in this paragraph is "marketplace". The intention of
the _American_ anti-trust legislation is to ensure that there is a fair
marketplace - in order to protect consumers. I do agree that MS
marketing would steamroller DEC/Compaq's non-existent marketing even in
a fair market, but we should always maintain the market in such a state
that if HP suddenly pulled up the socks of VMS marketing then they would
have a fair chance of being successful.

Mark
(A New Zealander)


