Feds' suit against Microsoft (and this is a common political practice,
by the way) is that Gates was approached by both the RNC and the DNC
for campaign contributions, to which he politely replied "no thanks".
He was then informed, in well-chosen words, that his company would not
be a target of investigation or subjected to an IRS audit if he were
to make hefty contributions (read "pay protection money").  When his
answer was still "no thanks" after that threat, Microsoft was
immediately demonized by the Feds (dutifully reported as such by a
lazy and/or biased press) and the DOJ, which is mostly "department"
with only trace elements of "justice", was given the green light to
send in the troops. This is evident by the fact that they based their
anti-trust argument on a very weak browser issue (which also means any
IRS audit they may have considered was even weaker).

By definition, a monopoly is when the public is not given another
choice.  Well, we do have other choices ... VMS, UNIX, Linux ... and
any Windows user can go to shareware.com and get Netscape or some
other browser for free.  The Feds know this, of course, and they knew
going into this suit that they'd probably lose.  However, they
achieved their ultimate goal ... they demonstrated that no one is
going to be immune from their fund-raising shake-down tactics.  I hope
Bill doesn't cave in and dole out contributions in the next election
cycle.  DOJ investigations and IRS audits on successful companies who
don't contribute to either or both of these incredibly corrupt parties
has to stop, and now is as good a time as any.


