Once upon a time, Jan Vorbrueggen said:
>
>politics2000@hotmail.com (politics2000@hotmail.com) writes:
>
>> By definition, a monopoly is when the public is not given another
>> choice.  Well, we do have other choices ... VMS, UNIX, Linux ... and
>> any Windows user can go to shareware.com and get Netscape or some
>> other browser for free.
>
>You might as well say "we can go and write our own compiler, operating 
>system and browser to be completely Microsoft-free". 

  Well, yes.  We have done that.  I have been 100% microsoft-free for 
many years, running gcc, gdb, perl, freebsd, linux, XFree86, KDE, 
Netscape, Lynx, Mutt, et al.  The only software I've wanted which I 
haven't been able to find for free is something with the same kind of 
functionality as MS-Project (though, I would prefer something that 
doesn't suck as badly as MS-Project).

  Just what, exactly, is the basis for your contention that Microsoft 
is a monopoly, given the existence of bountiful alternatives?  That 
people *think* they have no choice?

>The important point 
>is that Microsoft forbids the OEMs to set up the computers they sell in 
>such a way that their customers could make an easy, boot-time choice of 
>operating system.

  Microsoft cannot forbid this.  They can only use the techniques we 
have already seen (admittedly nonexhaustive list follows):
  1) they can design their licenses such that this is more expensive.
  2) they can design their product to make this practice difficult 
(qv, Windows95 detecting + reformatting HPFS partitions, overwriting 
boot managers (if installed after the boot manager), et al).
  3) they can stop returning the OEM's phone calls.
  4) they can use pervasive marketing/advertising and "embraced and 
extended" standard formats/protocols to fool people into thinking 
that they don't want to use anything but Microsoft products (or, on 
a related note, to fool OEM's into thinking that there isn't enough 
interest in non-Microsoft products to make the effort worth their 
while).

  This is "dirty pool", but well within their rights.  It might not 
be possible to establish legal checks against these practices without 
also preventing other companies from performing similar acts for more 
benign reasons (ie, for purely technical reasons).  Therefore we are 
probably better off not trying, and instead exploiting Microsoft's 
most severe weaknesses -- its lack of foresight and the low quality 
of its products -- to carve out pieces of the market for our own 
products.

  The situation's analogous to gun control legislation -- every 
attempt to curb firearm abuses with legal barriers to ownership has 
instead made that abuse easier and more pervasive by decreasing the 
numbers of law-abiding gun bearers.  Free of any fear of reprisal, 
criminals can use their weapons more boldly and to better effect. 
Also, the weakening of the gun culture results in a lower quality 
of gun education among children, resulting in greater numbers of 
accidents.

  Similarly, antimonopoly litigation against Microsoft will probably 
do little more than render Microsoft's would-be competitors less able 
to function effectively as software companies, and thus less able to 
compete against Microsoft.

  -- TTK



