HP, Compaq face a rough road
Ex-Digital workers in the region again in limbo, fear layoffs
By Ross Kerber, Globe Staff, 9/5/2001

LITTLETON -- When Compaq Computer Corp. acquired Digital Equipment Corp.
in 1998, the Houston-based PC maker hoped it was buying a company that
would prove to be the jewel in its crown.

Yesterday, though, Compaq executives were hardly bragging about their
success with the former Digital assets, whose performance fell short of
offsetting slowing PC sales for the number two computer maker.

Instead, Compaq said it has agreed to be acquired by Hewlett-Packard
Co., for stock worth about $20 billion.

Former Digital employees had hoped that the Compaq acquisition would
secure their future. But for the second time in three years, they find
themselves facing uncertainty.

To cut costs the companies said they would eliminate 15,000 jobs out of
a combined work force of more than 145,000 -- darkening the mood among
many of Compaq's 7,000 New England employees at facilities including
those in Lexington, Marlborough, and Nashua.

"It was a shock. It was like, `Here we go again,"' said an employee who
was walking into a Compaq office building on Taylor Street in Littleton.

After Compaq bought Digital, it eliminated about half the Massachusetts
work force. Now, the employee said, "We have to expect the same."

Hewlett-Packard and Compaq didn't say exactly where the ax would fall,
and they added that the cuts would take place through 2004.

But Compaq's New England divisions can expect major changes under the
new regime. For one thing, Compaq's data-storage division, with workers
in Shrewsbury and Littleton, competes directly with the former
operations of StorageApps Inc. of Bridgewater, N.J., which
Hewlett-Packard bought in July.

Also, while Compaq ran much of its vaunted services business -- most of
which it acquired in the Digital deal -- out of Massachusetts, those
employees will now report to Hewlett-Packard executive Ann Livermore,
who is based in Mountain View, Calif. Compaq's current sales-and-service
chief, Peter Blackmoor, will be assigned to run another division.

"The key indicator of what will be left of Compaq-Digital in
Massachusetts will be to see who ultimately takes responsibility for
those pieces," said Kirby Wadsworth, a former marketing vice president
at Digital and Compaq who is now at a start-up data-storage company,
Storability Inc., in Southborough.

The acquisition, Wadsworth said, could be a boon for some workers.

Hewlett-Packard is known for its focus on engineering, and many former
Digital employees might find it a better cultural fit than with Compaq,
which put a greater emphasis on sales and marketing. "This will be the
cultural reverse of Compaq's acquisition of Digital," Wadsworth said.

The state of Compaq's services division illustrates the troubles Compaq
had with the Digital purchase. Compaq had hoped the deal would yield big
corporate service accounts, with higher margins than Compaq's original
PC business had. But those plans were never realized.

Digital's service chief, John Rando, left the organization in the middle
of 1999, and his responsibilities were split among several other
executives. None could quite meet investor hopes that Compaq could
capture business from service leaders like IBM Corp. Compaq's services
revenue rose to $3.4 billion from $3.3 billion during the first six
months of this year, hardly enough to make up for a decline in product
revenue to $14.2 billion from $16.3 billion in the same period.

"Compaq bought Digital for services," Ian Bertram, a regional director
at Gartner Group in Singapore, told Bloomberg News. "They haven't made a
particularly good showing of services. Now HP buys Compaq -- how much
baggage is going to come with this?"

Compaq public-relations executives wouldn't permit local executives or
employees to be interviewed yesterday, and at one point a security guard
asked a reporter to leave Compaq's Taylor Street office park in
Littleton. Several workers who wouldn't give their names said they were
wary of more layoffs but felt Compaq perhaps had to sell out.

"People are sort of taking it in stride," said an engineer who was
leaving a Compaq office on King Street. The acquisition is "something
you have to deal with in the global economy."

This engineer said he had considered joining several friends who left
Compaq to join dot-com start-ups in recent years. But some of these
people have already lost their jobs, amid a general decline in the
technology sector.

Big rewards and tremendous uncertainty are both part of the technology
sector, the engineer suggested.

"It cuts both ways," he said.


