Sept. 5, 2001, 11:52AM

Compaq-HP deal receives wary reception
Workers fear for jobs; shares plunge
By TOM FOWLER
Copyright 2001 Houston Chronicle

From Texas 249 to Wall Street, Compaq Corp. employees and investors
alike are wondering whether this week's deal with Hewlett-Packard is one
they can live with.

Houston employees of the computer giant, already battered by several
recent waves of job cuts, worried Tuesday whether they would be next --
and if not, what form their new company would take.

Investors, meanwhile, reacted negatively to the deal Tuesday, driving
down shares of Compaq and Hewlett-Packard 10 percent and 18 percent,
respectively.

By the end of the day, the deal, which started out valued at $25
billion, had fallen nearly $5 billion.

Despite the concerns, company executives cast the deal in as bright a
light as possible for Houston.

"We will be No. 9 on the Fortune 500 list and will have an impressive
reach," said Compaq Chief Executive Officer Michael Capellas, who would
be the president of the combined companies. "In time, market conditions
will improve and there's no doubt we will eventually be growing the
company."

Carly Fiorina, chief executive officer of the Palo Alto, Calif.,
company, said California's energy woes and the general cost of doing
business in the Silicon Valley also may bode well for Houston's
operations in the long term.

"From the point of view of power, the cost of living, education and
transportation infrastructure, Houston offers us some great benefits,"
Fiorina said.

Whatever happens, company executives said Tuesday, Compaq's Houston
campus would be home to the new company's Intel-based computer and
server products, as well as much of the new product research and
development.

But with so many overlapping businesses between the two companies,
particularly in desktop and notebook PCs, analysts say they expect the
companies will have many redundant operations that will need to be cut
before closing the deal.

"This is a deal where one plus one equals one and-a-half," said Todd
Kort, an analyst with Gartner Dataquest, a technology consulting
company. "There's so much overlap in business between HP and Compaq that
you've got to wonder if much of Compaq is actually going to remain in
two to three years time."

As part of the merger, which is expected to close by the middle of 2002,
subject to approval by regulators and stockholders, the companies plan
to cut a combined 15,000 workers from their current rosters -- on top of
the layoffs currently under way at both companies.

With layoffs completed, the combined company would have about 135,000
employees in 164 countries, Capellas said during a news conference
Tuesday.

Compaq already has announced 8,500 layoffs worldwide from its 66,000
employees, including 2,800 in Houston. Hewlett-Packard announced plans
in recent months to cut 9,000 from its 87,000-strong workforce.

Where the company would cut jobs has not been announced and still isn't
clear, even to company insiders. But Compaq's Houston operations are
likely to see job cuts in the hundreds, if not a thousand or more.

Compaq employees and contractors said the mood at the company's
northwest Harris County campus on Texas 249 was somber.

"It's like a ghost town," said Brad Gray, a contractor who works on
Compaq's internal computer network. "People are mostly staying in their
offices."

Gray said that when his particular contract is up, he doesn't expect to
still be working at Compaq.

"The company I actually work for is a competitor of HP," he said. "I
don't think my contract will be renewed six months from now."

One employee said there was a "lot of huddling" in hallways, and that
people were in shock. Others were talking -- some sardonically -- about
possibly making the move to Palo Alto.

"There's a feeling that we put up the white flag, that we surrendered
and told HP, `Come and take us,' " the employee said.

Some Compaq employees learned of the merger from the news media, rather
than from the company.

Agnes Gerlich, who manages a Shipley Do-Nut Shop across from the Compaq
campus, said her early-morning customers -- many of them Compaq
employees -- pulled up short when they spotted the Houston Chronicle box
outside the store's door and saw the front-page headlines.

The combination of the two companies would create a business with a
broad product line that, in some ways, surpasses even that of IBM, said
Ashok Kumar, an analyst with US Bancorp Piper Jaffray. They range from
Compaq's fault-tolerant Himalaya machines, which are used to run most of
the world's stock markets, to desktop and notebook computers and
hand-held computers.

The company also would have a large professional services business.

However, the companies overlap significantly in PCs, namely desktop
computers and notebook computers used by individuals and businesses, as
well as industry standard servers, the lower-end machines used to power
Web sites or smaller company networks.

Compaq's server products generally are considered better than
Hewlett-Packard's, so it makes sense that servers would be based in
Houston.

Hewlett-Packard's name would be on all the products of the combined
company, but some Compaq brand names, such as Proliant for its line of
servers, or iPaq for its successful hand-held computer, might remain.

When consumers walk into a computer retail store next year, however,
they are more likely to see computers that look more like the current
Hewlett-Packard machines than the designs Compaq currently has.

Analysts are less than enthusiastic about the deal, however, saying it
ultimately creates more questions than answers.

"The two CEOs are falling into each other's arms because both are
hurting right now," Kort said. "But if you look at the longer-term
perspective, it doesn't get them very much in their fight against Dell
or IBM."

Compaq's professional services division undoubtedly was a major draw for
Hewlett-Packard, since that business typically has higher profit margins
than making and selling hardware. Hewlett-Packard made an $18 billion
bid for accounting and consulting giant PricewaterhouseCoopers last year
to shore up its consulting business, but that deal fell through.

"It would have been better for both companies to find a few more
services businesses to buy and get out of Dell's way," Kort said.

Kumar predicts that Dell Computer Corp. would be the main beneficiary of
the merger as the Round Rock-based company has better operating margins
compared with Hewlett-Packard and Compaq. Gateway, the smaller, San
Diego-based computer maker, most likely would feel squeezed by the
larger competitors.

The ongoing layoffs in Compaq's Houston operations actually might spur
more start-up business activity, said Paul Frison, president and chief
executive officer of the Houston Technology Center, a nonprofit tech
industry support group.

"There hasn't been a outward migration of people from Houston since
layoffs started because this is still the place to be for doing
business," Frison said. "I think we'll see more people having lost their
jobs looking at starting their own businesses rather than uproot
themselves from Houston."

The merged company's local presence also might expand once
Hewlett-Packard is exposed to the business potential in Houston, Frison
said.

"This hasn't been a company that creates technology just for
technology's sake, but rather we've developed it to use in day-to-day
business, like at NASA, at the Medical Center and in the energy
industry," Frison said, referring to Compaq. "It could ultimately mean a
heck of a lot more business for Hewlett-Packard here."

While the companies wait for a formal review of the merger plan by U.S.
and European antitrust officials, they will begin to merge their
redundant operations, said Bill Porter, managing partner of Houston law
firm Porter & Hedges, who follows Compaq and is familiar with the
issues.

Merging the two cultures might not be as difficult as in some mergers,
given Compaq's recent history of acquisitions and the fact that both
companies have seen an influx of new management in recent years, Porter
said.

"If nothing else, this will certainly put the romance back in the lives
of Compaq shareholders," Porter said.

However, shares of Hewlett-Packard plunged $4.21 on Tuesday to close at
$19.00, an 18 percent decline. Compaq's stock also fell, down just over
10 percent to $11.08.



