percent in the past year; Compaq has dropped 68 percent.

Late Monday, Hewlett Packard, the second-biggest computer maker, announced it
would buy Compaq, the second-biggest personal computer maker, in an all stock
transaction originally valued at $25 billion that has declined since to $18.8
billion. Investors dumped shares of both companies yesterday, doubting
Hewlett-Packard Chief Executive Carly Fiorina's bet that the bigger company
can sell more services to lift profit as personal-computer demand slumps.


