> 
> What will happen to Compaq when US recovers?  Dell has cut less than a
> thousand jobs, but Compaq...
> 
> From:
>   http://bostonworks.boston.com/globe/articles/090201_cuts.html
> 
> Analysts say recent job cuts too deep
> Firms may lose out when US recovers
> By Robert Dodge, The Dallas Morning News, 9/2/01
> 
> WASHINGTON � Economic troubles often force companies to cut the fat � and
> that includes excess employees.
> 
> But some specialists think corporations are going too far. A parade of big
> corporate job cuts � nearly 1 million announced so far this year � suggests
> companies are cutting beyond the fat and into the muscle, leaving them less
> able to spring back when growth resumes.

I've been saying that for a good ten years, at least: that companies
need to learn the difference between "lean and mean" and "feeble and
anorexic".

A good chunk of corporate America falls into that latter category.

Reference: Steven Covey - "production vs. production capability".

<rant>
I guess you have to be a Harvard Biz grad. to understand how cutting
"costs" (read: "ability to produce") can actually HELP your ability to
profit. Seems to my simple mind that it's hard to reap a profit from
products you're not able to produce due to cuts. But, then, if you can't
mark something up ten times or more, then that product is not
"profitable" (read: "usuriously priced") - so, cut it. Makes you wonder
why anyone bothers to stay in the PC business, huh?

I've wondered for some time now: everyone wants to see the ecomony
continue to grow: profits go up meaning prices go up meaning wages go up
meaning costs go up meaning prices go up meaning profits go up meaning
prices go up meaning wages go up meaning costs go up meaning prices go
up meaning ...

Where does it end? (Remember 1929? ...the thirties?) Where is "terminal
'velocity'"? ...and what happens the ecomony gets so big it can't grow
anymore? (Remember 1929? ...the thirties?)

What will industry do when there are no more "third world" countries to
exploit for cheap labor? My brother (blue collar, like my father before
us) looks at the endless miles of office buildings as asks, "how many
paper shufflers does the world really need?" Can you show me any common
household goods or items of apparel that are made in the USA? Does that
even mean anything anymore?

No one wants to build "affordable" (there's THAT *WORD* again!) housing,
everyone wants to live in an "upscale" community free from "crime"
(better you should commit white collar crimes at work than commit common
crimes at home in your own community, I guess).

Where does the blue collar work force live? Where can they afford to
buy?

Geez - I must need a shrink, huh?
</rant>

