I find this highly unlikely.  IA64 (the architecture) is largely HP's
design, the second implementation (McKinley) will be HP's, and they
already have a fallback position--PA-RISC.  Given their level of
commitment to IA64, I can't imagine them wanting alpha as a fallback
position.  I am surprised HP didn't want the DEQ patents and other
intellectual property, but perhaps Intel insisted they needed it.

> Don't know if replying to your own posts is ok, but another point to
> consider is that HP have always been a technology / R&D driven
> company.

HP used to be a engineering-driven company.  That culture has been
weakening in recent years, and much of the remaining "HP Way" seems to
have gone when the test equipment and measurement business was spun
off into Agilent.

> Have used their test equipment for years and it was and probably
> still is some of most bulletproof and the best engineered kit you
> can buy.

Yep, and HP doesn't make it anymore--all that is now Agilent.  The
company with the HP name is primarily a manufacture of computer
systems, printers and network gear.  They do have some very good
engineering talent, but the company as a whole isn't what it used
to be.
